Waterloo MCP server

This package exposes read-only access to Waterloo data roots over MCP.

Start here if you do not know the server yet:
- use `about('waterloo.introduction')` for a short project overview
- use `about('waterlint.command')` for validation and diagnostics workflows
- use `about('waterloo.structure')` for docstring shape, sections, and scope rules
- use `about('waterloo.markup')` for inline markup roles and tokens
- use the `inspect_root` prompt before drilling into individual objects
- use the `inspect_object` prompt once you know the object you want to inspect
- use search tools after you have a rough idea of the root or object you need

Current focus:
- list configured roots
- read one configured root by its `root_id`
- list the objects in one configured root by its `root_id`
- read one object by `root_id` and `qid`
- read one stored section by `root_id`, `qid`, and section name
- read one stored subsection by `root_id`, `qid`, section name, and subsection name
- search objects by expression and structural filters
- search section and subsection labels by expression and structural filters,
  returning compact location records without redundant match text
- inspect the structured See_also neighborhood around one object
- inspect the indexed Waterloo data behind them
- keep the server simple and stdio-based for local development
- offer Streamable HTTP for Inspector-based inspection at `/mcp`

The current entry point is `wtrl_mcp`.
The Inspector-friendly entry point is `sdv.doc.waterloo.mcp.wtrl_server:runner`.

Typical usage:
- use `--config` to select a TOML file explicitly
- or keep the prepared TOML files under `etc/` and pass one of them via `--config`
- use `--gen-config-template` to print a template to stdout
- configure `transport = "stdio"` or `transport = "streamable-http"` in the
  TOML file
- use `--port` to override the internal bind port when starting the server
- use `--public-port` only when the externally visible port differs from the
  bind port; it is used for host allowlists and generated URLs
- use `--allowed-hosts` to override the host allowlist; bare host names are
  normalized to the active port automatically
- when regenerating the MCP doc-JSON from docstrings, use the package root
  `package_main/src/sdv/doc/waterloo/mcp` as `--basedir` and address the
  modules relative to that directory, for example `--obj wtrl_server`
  instead of `--obj mcp.server`; this avoids name clashes with the Python
  `mcp` SDK package and makes local chat-driven testing easier
- browser Inspector example:
  - set `transport = "streamable-http"` in the TOML file
  - set `allowed_origins = ["http://gilgamesh:6274"]`
  - set `allowed_hosts = ["127.0.0.1:8000"]`
  - connect the Inspector to `http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp`
- inspect `tools/list`
- call `describe_tool` with a canonical tool name
- call `list_roots`
- call `get_root` with a `root_id`
- call `get_root_metadata` with a `root_id` when you only need the compact header metadata
- call `list_objects` with a `root_id`
- call `get_object` with a `root_id` and `qid`
- call `get_section` with a `root_id`, `qid`, and section name
- call `get_subsection` with a `root_id`, `qid`, section name, and subsection name
- call `get_signature` with a `root_id` and `qid`
- call `get_references` with a `root_id` and `qid`
- call `get_examples` with a `root_id` and `qid`
- call `get_example_source` with a `root_id` and canonical example path
- call `search_objects` with an expression and an optional filter object
- call `search_sections` with an expression and an optional filter object
- call `search_text` with a list of terms and an optional filter object
- call `search_related` with a `root_id` and `qid`

Choosing a tool (response size matters):
- to locate objects, prefer `search_objects`, `search_sections`, or
  `search_text`, then read targeted detail with `get_object`, `get_section`,
  or `get_subsection`
- prefer `get_root_metadata` over `get_root` when you only need the compact
  header metadata
- `get_root` returns the full document body for one root and can be very
  large; call it only when you genuinely need the complete document. The
  `MCPS-005` guardrail may reject oversized roots.

Pytest workflow:
- the MCP HTTP integration tests live in `package_main/pytest/pytest_mcp_*.py`
- they expect a running MCP server on `http://127.0.0.1:13316/mcp`
- if the server is not running, the tests skip with a short start-up hint
- `pytest_mcp_http.py` exercises `tools/list` and `gen_docstring` against the
  canonical JSON templates in `package_main/templates-json/out`
- the tests intentionally use the live server so that the `streamable-http`
  wire behavior stays close to the actual browser/Inspector workflow

Lookup failures are currently reported as standardized tool messages with
`MCPS-001` through `MCPS-007` prefixes, for example unknown `root_id`,
unknown `qid`, unknown section and subsection labels, an unknown example
reference, or an unknown tool name. `MCPS-005` is reserved for the root-size
guardrail on `get_root`.

The text-vs-structured error question is still open; see
`package_main/doc/source/mcp.rst` for the normative current status.

The `root_id` is a short stable hash of the canonical absolute root path.

The server is intentionally read-only.
